Rule 1 says vulnerabilities require user-controlled input reaching dangerous sinks. But rule 2 says hardcoded literal credentials (key/secret/password/token) are themselves vulnerabilities. So CWE-798 here qualifies as a vulnerability even without user-controlled input flowing to a sink — the hardcoded key IS the vulnerability.

This matches the background: "Dromara Sureness before v1.0.8 was discovered to use a hardcoded key."

Impact: Since Sureness is an open-source library, the default HMAC secret key is publicly known. Any deployment that doesn't call `setDefaultSecretKey()` will sign/verify JWTs with this publicly-known key. An attacker can forge arbitrary JWT tokens (with any subject, roles, perms claims), leading to complete authentication bypass and privilege escalation. That's Critical severity.

